    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Vitamin K2 has modest evidence for slowing bone loss in postmenopausal women, and almost none for preventing fractures or protecting arteries.</strong> The strongest trial gave 180mcg of MK-7 daily for three years to 244 healthy postmenopausal women and slowed the decline at the lumbar spine and femoral neck, but not the total hip.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Where it works:</strong> pooled across 16 trials and 6,425 people, K2 helped lumbar spine density; hip, femoral neck and forearm showed no significant difference.</li>
        <li><strong>The studied dose:</strong> 180mcg of MK-7 a day for three years. Most Indian labels carry 45mcg or 100mcg.</li>
        <li><strong>India context:</strong> ICMR-NIN sets 55mcg a day for vitamin K overall, and a 2022 Indian pilot found MK-7 undetectable in the staple foods it tested.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limit:</strong> fracture results are inconsistent, the artery claim is mechanism not evidence, and Skelecoll contains no vitamin K2.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

