      <h2 id="density">Does vitamin K2 increase bone density?</h2>

      <p>
        It slows loss at some measurement sites in postmenopausal women and does nothing measurable
        at others. "Increase" is the wrong verb for what the trials found; "decline more slowly" is
        closer. The mechanism is not in dispute — vitamin K activates osteocalcin, which binds
        calcium into bone, and matrix Gla protein, which blocks calcium depositing in vessel walls —
        but a nutrient can activate a protein and still fail to move a bone scan.
      </p>

      <p>
        The clearest single trial is Knapen et al. in <em>Osteoporosis International</em>, 2013:
        244 healthy postmenopausal women randomised to 180mcg of MK-7 daily or placebo for three
        years. MK-7 significantly reduced the age-related decline in bone mineral density and
        calculated bone strength at the lumbar spine and femoral neck — but not at the total hip.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pooling narrows it further. A 2022 meta-analysis in <em>Frontiers in Public Health</em>
        combined 16 randomised controlled trials and 6,425 participants: K2 improved lumbar spine
        density, with no significant difference at the hip, femoral neck or forearm. A later
        <em>Bone &amp; Joint Research</em> meta-analysis found a lower-spine gain of 0.01 g/cm²,
        with wide variation between trials. And there are flat nulls: RenaKvit gave 360mcg of MK-7
        daily for two years to 123 dialysis patients and found no bone benefit, and a Norwegian
        trial of natto-derived K2 found none over a year. The effect is not reliable enough to show
        up in every population.
      </p>

