        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="fractures">Does vitamin K2 prevent fractures?</h2>

      <p>
        Nobody can honestly say yes yet. Fractures are the outcome that matters and the one these
        trials were least designed to measure.
      </p>

      <p>
        The ECKO trial, <em>PLoS Medicine</em> 2008, randomised 440 postmenopausal women with
        osteopenia to 5mg of vitamin K1 daily or placebo for two to four years. Its bone density
        result was flatly negative; its fracture count pointed the other way, 9 women fracturing on
        vitamin K against 20 on placebo — and its authors called for more research rather than
        claiming the effect. Pooled reviews have not settled it either: the 2022 <em>Frontiers</em>
        analysis notes fracture was not the primary outcome of the trials available, and a large
        Japanese trial of the 45mg MK-4 dose did not reduce new spine fractures overall across three
        years.
      </p>

      <p>
        So if your reason for taking K2 is to avoid breaking a hip, the evidence does not support
        it. <a href="/pages/how-to-increase-bone-density">Loading the skeleton</a> and your
        <a href="/pages/osteopenia-vs-osteoporosis">DEXA and FRAX result</a> are where those
        decisions actually sit.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mk4-mk7">MK-4 or MK-7, and how much vitamin K2 should you take?</h2>

      <p>
        The two forms are not interchangeable, and the dose gap between them is the most misread
        fact in the category. MK-4's bone evidence comes from a 45mg prescription dose; MK-7's from
        180mcg, roughly 250 times smaller.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">MK-4 (menatetrenone)</th><th scope="col">MK-7 (menaquinone-7)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Studied dose</th><td>45mg a day</td><td>180mcg a day</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Status</th><td>Prescription osteoporosis drug in Japan since 1995</td><td>Food supplement worldwide</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Best outcome data</th><td>Fracture trials, mixed — a large trial found no overall reduction in new spine fractures over three years</td><td>Bone density and calculated bone strength over three years; no fracture trial</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Half-life</th><td>Short, so the drug dose is split through the day</td><td>Long, so one daily capsule is plausible</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Head-to-head trial</th><td colspan="2">None exists, and no validated conversion makes the two regimens equivalent</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        So "vitamin K2 (MK-7) 100mcg" sits a little under the only long-term bone trial, while
        "vitamin K2 (MK-4) 100mcg" is not a gentle version of the Japanese drug regimen — it is
        0.2% of it, and that amount of MK-4 has never been tested for bone. Read the form and the
        number together, or the figure tells you nothing.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-quote">
        {%- if settings.founder_1_image != blank -%}
          <img src="{{ settings.founder_1_image | image_url: width: 112 }}" alt="Dr Nachiket Pansey" width="56" height="56" loading="lazy">
