          <cite><strong>Dr Nachiket Pansey</strong> — Orthopaedic Surgeon, Founder of Dr Collagen</cite>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Safety is not the constraint. The US Institute of Medicine could not set a tolerable upper
        intake level for vitamin K because high intakes produced no adverse effects to base one on,
        and in 2025 the Council for Responsible Nutrition set a highest observed intake of 375mcg
        for supplemental MK-7 after reviewing more than 40 trials. That is an observation about
        harm, not a reason to take more: the bone data sits at 180mcg, and the CRN figure excludes
        anyone on a vitamin K antagonist.
      </p>

      <h2 id="india">Do you need vitamin K2 supplements on an Indian diet?</h2>

      <p>
        Possibly more than a Japanese or European adult does, because the dietary route to K2
        barely exists here. Whether that absence matters clinically is still unproven.
      </p>

      <p>
        Vitamin K1 is the easy half: it comes from green leafy vegetables, and regular palak, methi,
        sarson or coriander supplies it readily against the ICMR-NIN 2020 figure of 55mcg a day for
        adults — itself below the 90 to 120mcg adequate intakes used in the United States. K2 is the
        harder half, because the richest food source anywhere is natto, a Japanese fermented
        soyabean dish that is not part of Indian eating.
      </p>

      <p>
        A pilot study in <em>JMIR Public Health and Surveillance</em>, 2022, measured serum vitamin
        K1 and MK-7 by HPLC in 209 patients with type 2 diabetes and 50 healthy volunteers, and
        tested common Indian staple foods. Serum K1 was normal in both groups. Serum MK-7 sat in a
        low band of roughly 0.3 to 0.4 ng/mL, and the staple foods contained no detectable MK-7 at
        all, their K1 ranging from 0 to 37mcg per 100g.
      </p>

      <p>
        Read that as carefully as its authors did. They call it insufficiency rather than deficiency,
        note that ordinary Indian intake is probably enough for vitamin K's blood-clotting role, and
        say their results suggest but do not prove it is common — a small pilot using an MK-7
        threshold they themselves call poorly established. It makes K2 a fair question for an Indian
        adult. It does not make it a proven gap.
      </p>

      <h2 id="skelecoll">Is vitamin K2 in Skelecoll?</h2>

      <p>
        No. Skelecoll contains no vitamin K2, in any form, at any dose. You would not learn that
        from most bone supplement pages, so it is worth stating plainly on ours.
      </p>

      <p>
        What it carries for bone is 250mg of calcium, 600 IU of vitamin D3 and 50mg of magnesium
        alongside 10,000mg of hydrolysed marine collagen. The calcium is a contribution towards the
        1,000mg Indian adults are asked to reach, not the whole of it, and the D3 addresses the
        deficiency that actually governs calcium absorption — in India far the commoner problem, and
        the subject of <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">our vitamin D article</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Whether that absence is a real gap depends on what you think the lumbar-spine finding is
        worth. Our reading: one site out of four, no fracture evidence, and a systematic review of
        nine randomised trials finding vitamin K does not consistently slow arterial calcification
        either — so the "steers calcium into bone, away from arteries" diagram is a mechanism, not a
        result. That does not yet justify adding an ingredient. If you and your doctor want K2, it is
        sold separately as MK-7 at 180mcg a day, a number to hold against the label in front of you
        exactly as you would <a href="/pages/how-much-collagen-per-day">hold a collagen dose against
        the trial doses</a>.
      </p>

