      <h2 id="doctor">Who should not take vitamin K2, and when to see a doctor</h2>

      <p>
        One group must not start K2 on their own. If you take warfarin, acenocoumarol or any other
        vitamin K antagonist, vitamin K directly opposes how the drug works: it lowers your INR, and
        a lower INR than your prescriber calibrated means a higher clotting risk. Stopping abruptly
        destabilises things just as much as starting, because your drug dose was set around what you
        were already taking. Never start or stop it without telling the doctor who manages your
        INR.
      </p>

      <p>Book an appointment rather than adding a supplement if you have:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>A fracture from a minor fall or knock, especially at the wrist, hip or spine.</li>
        <li>Height loss, a new stoop, or mid-back pain that began without an injury.</li>
        <li>Early menopause, or periods that stopped well before the usual age.</li>
        <li>Long-term steroid use, chronic kidney disease, coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, or bariatric surgery — all of which affect how you handle fat-soluble vitamins.</li>
        <li>Any prescription anticoagulant, so the decision is recorded rather than assumed.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="jr-disclaimer">
        This article is for general information and is not medical advice. Nutritional supplements
        are not intended to diagnose, treat, cure or prevent any disease. If you are pregnant,
        breastfeeding, taking prescription medication, or have a known fish or shellfish allergy,
        speak to a qualified healthcare professional before starting supplementation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need to take vitamin K2 with vitamin D3?</summary>
          <p>No trial has shown that you must. The pairing is sold on mechanism: vitamin D helps you absorb calcium, vitamin K activates the proteins that handle it. But the trials testing whether K2 adds anything to vitamin D for bone or artery outcomes have mostly been null, and vitamin D works on calcium absorption without it. Correcting a measured vitamin D deficiency is the step with real evidence behind it.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much vitamin K2 should I take for my bones?</summary>
          <p>If you decide to take it, 180mcg a day of MK-7 is the dose with three-year bone data behind it, from a trial in 244 postmenopausal women. Many Indian labels carry 45mcg or 100mcg, which is below that. There is no evidence that going above 180mcg does more for bone, so a bigger number on a label is not a better product.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does vitamin K2 prevent fractures?</summary>
          <p>The evidence does not support that expectation. The ECKO trial in 440 postmenopausal women with osteopenia recorded 9 fractures on vitamin K against 20 on placebo, but its bone density result was negative and the authors called the fracture finding preliminary. Pooled reviews note fracture was not the primary outcome of the available trials, so the effect cannot be assessed accurately.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take vitamin K2 if I am on warfarin or acenocoumarol?</summary>
          <p>Not without your prescriber's involvement. Vitamin K directly opposes how these drugs work, lowers your INR and can raise your clotting risk. Stopping a K2 supplement suddenly destabilises things as much as starting one, because your drug dose was calibrated around your existing intake. Raise it with the doctor who manages your INR before changing anything.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does Skelecoll contain vitamin K2?</summary>
          <p>No. Skelecoll contains no vitamin K2 in any form. For bone it supplies 250mg calcium, 600 IU vitamin D3 and 50mg magnesium alongside 10,000mg of hydrolysed marine collagen. We have not added K2 because its pooled benefit appears at one measurement site out of four, with no fracture evidence and null artery trials.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I get enough vitamin K2 from an Indian vegetarian diet?</summary>
          <p>Vitamin K1 yes, K2 probably not. Palak, methi and sarson supply K1 readily against the ICMR-NIN target of 55mcg a day for adults. K2 is different: the richest source is natto, which is not an Indian food, and a 2022 Indian pilot study found no detectable MK-7 in the staple foods it tested. Whether that absence matters clinically is still unproven.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

