        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Do I need to take vitamin K2 with vitamin D3?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No trial has shown that you must. The pairing is sold on mechanism: vitamin D helps you absorb calcium, vitamin K activates the proteins that handle it. But the trials testing whether K2 adds anything to vitamin D for bone or artery outcomes have mostly been null, and vitamin D works on calcium absorption without it. Correcting a measured vitamin D deficiency is the step with real evidence behind it." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How much vitamin K2 should I take for my bones?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "If you decide to take it, 180mcg a day of MK-7 is the dose with three-year bone data behind it, from a trial in 244 postmenopausal women. Many Indian labels carry 45mcg or 100mcg, which is below that. There is no evidence that going above 180mcg does more for bone, so a bigger number on a label is not a better product." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Does vitamin K2 prevent fractures?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The evidence does not support that expectation. The ECKO trial in 440 postmenopausal women with osteopenia recorded 9 fractures on vitamin K against 20 on placebo, but its bone density result was negative and the authors called the fracture finding preliminary. Pooled reviews note fracture was not the primary outcome of the available trials, so the effect cannot be assessed accurately." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Can I take vitamin K2 if I am on warfarin or acenocoumarol?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Not without your prescriber's involvement. Vitamin K directly opposes how these drugs work, lowers your INR and can raise your clotting risk. Stopping a K2 supplement suddenly destabilises things as much as starting one, because your drug dose was calibrated around your existing intake. Raise it with the doctor who manages your INR before changing anything." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Does Skelecoll contain vitamin K2?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Skelecoll contains no vitamin K2 in any form. For bone it supplies 250mg calcium, 600 IU vitamin D3 and 50mg magnesium alongside 10,000mg of hydrolysed marine collagen. We have not added K2 because its pooled benefit appears at one measurement site out of four, with no fracture evidence and null artery trials." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Can I get enough vitamin K2 from an Indian vegetarian diet?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Vitamin K1 yes, K2 probably not. Palak, methi and sarson supply K1 readily against the ICMR-NIN target of 55mcg a day for adults. K2 is different: the richest source is natto, which is not an Indian food, and a 2022 Indian pilot study found no detectable MK-7 in the staple foods it tested. Whether that absence matters clinically is still unproven." } }
